<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Edo Castle</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1457">1457</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Edo Castle was a flatland castle in Edo, modern Tokyo, first built by Ōta Dōkan in 1457. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a> made it his seat in 1590, and it was rebuilt on a vast scale as the residence of the Tokugawa <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shoguns</a> from 1603 to 1867, surrounded by moats and the mansions of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daimyo">daimyo</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-restoration">Meiji Restoration</a> it became the imperial residence, and its site is today the Tokyo Imperial Palace.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/edo-castle.jpg" alt="Edo Castle" width="320" height="217" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Edo_P_detail.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The warrior Edo Shigetsugu built a residence on the site of the later Honmaru and Ninomaru around the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heian-period">Heian period</a> or the start of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamakura">Kamakura</a> period. His clan left in the 15th century after uprisings in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanto-region">Kanto region</a>, and Ota Dokan, a retainer of the Ogigayatsu Uesugi family, built Edo Castle in 1457. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a> established the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa shogunate</a> there, and the castle was the residence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogun</a> and headquarters of the military government through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-period">Edo period</a> (1603-1867). It is also called Chiyoda Castle.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The plan was large and elaborate. The grounds were divided into wards separated by moats and large stone walls carrying keeps, defence houses and towers: the Honmaru at the centre, the Ninomaru and Sannomaru extending east, the Nishinomaru to the west and the Kitanomaru to the north. East of the Sannomaru an outer moat enclosed the districts of Otomachi and Daimyo-Koji. There were 36 major gates, and Ote-mon was the main one and the most heavily armed. The circumference is debated, with estimates from 6 to 10 miles.</p><p>The Spanish governor Rodrigo de Vivero y Velasco described the castle in 1608-1609, remarking on its huge stones and the crowds within: he claimed to have seen 20,000 servants between the first gate and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogun</a>'s palace, and to have passed two ranks of 1,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musket">musketeers</a>. The Honmaru held the keep and residence of the shogun; its main halls were said to cover 33,000 square metres in the Kan-ei era (1624-1644). Its five-storey keep, 51 metres high and the tallest castle tower in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, was built in 1607, rebuilt in 1623 and 1638, and destroyed in the Fire of Meireki of 1657 and never reconstructed. Fujimi-yagura took over its role from 1659.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Fires repeatedly destroyed the Honmaru, and after each one the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogun</a> normally moved into the Nishinomaru. The main palace was destroyed in 1863 and not rebuilt, and the Nishinomaru itself burned in 1853 and again on 5 May 1873, after which the imperial palace of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-era">Meiji era</a> was built on its site. The castle's corridor Matsu no Orōka was the scene in 1701 of the events that led to the forty-seven <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ronin">ronin</a> incident.</p><p>After the resignation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogun</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-restoration">Meiji Restoration</a> the castle became the Tokyo Imperial Palace. Some moats, walls and ramparts survive, including the Fujimi-yagura, the Fujimi-tamon and the Ote-mon, which took its present form in the 1620s, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/date-masamune">Date Masamune</a> helped with the repairs. The grounds in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-period">Edo period</a> were more extensive than today's, since Tokyo Station and the Marunouchi district lay inside the outermost moat. Many Tokyo place names derive from the castle, among them Otemachi, Toranomon and Marunouchi.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Edo Castle” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Harris Treaty of 1858, which opened further ports, granted extraterritoriality to foreigners and imposed low fixed tariffs, was concluded without imperial sanction and provoked fierce opposition; its architect, the chief minister Ii Naosuke, was assassinated outside <b>Edo Castle</b> in 1860.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa Shogunate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In <b>Edo Castle</b>’s women’s quarters (the Ōoku), Kasuga no Tsubone, wet nurse of the shogun Iemitsu, wielded influence likened to a senior councillor’s, and Otama, a grocer’s daughter, became mother of the fifth shogun, Tokugawa Tsunayoshi.</blockquote><a href="/societies/tokugawa-japan">Social Hierarchy of Tokugawa Japan</a></li><li><blockquote>Its opening words were changed in the Kamakura period, when samurai sang it as a festive song; in the Edo period it was popular among the people, and was sung as a New Year song in the Ōoku of <b>Edo Castle</b> and in the Satsuma domain.</blockquote><a href="/songs/kimigayo">Kimigayo</a></li><li><blockquote>Rōnin samurai of the Mito and Satsuma domains killed Ii Naosuke, Chief Minister of the Tokugawa shogunate, outside the Sakurada Gate of <b>Edo Castle</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-24">24 March in history</a></li></ul></section>
